<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  A job names a driver; the driver runs toils. This is the naming half.

  <driver> and <trainsSkill> are integers because they are JobIndex and SkillIndex values, which
  the published views and the save both carry. Naming them here instead would need a second table
  mapping a name to a compile-time constant, which is the sort of indirection that looks tidy and
  buys nothing until drivers are content — and they are not, because each one is a C# class.

  experiencePerWorkTick is ASSUMED at 110 for every working job. a-01-pawns.md gives the level
  costs and the passion multipliers but not the base rate per tick; at 110 a colonist who works
  two thirds of a day earns about 4,400 points, which is where SkillDef.dailySoftCap sits, and
  that relationship is the point of the cap.
-->
<Defs>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Haul</defName>
    <driver>0</driver>
    <expiryTicks>5000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>0</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Eat</defName>
    <driver>1</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <workTicks>300</workTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Sleep</defName>
    <driver>2</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Wander</defName>
    <driver>3</driver>
    <expiryTicks>1200</expiryTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Wait</defName>
    <driver>4</driver>
    <workTicks>120</workTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    800 ticks is the vanilla harvest work of the pine class, the wooded meadow's only species
    (a-08-plants-growing-food.md section 1). One tree per job, and the swings train cutting.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Fell</defName>
    <driver>5</driver>
    <workTicks>800</workTicks>
    <expiryTicks>6000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>1</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>


  <!--
    No workTicks: mining is priced per material, and the terrain defs already carry the number
    (rock 700, iron 900, coal 760). One constant here would make a seam cost the same as the
    stone around it, which is the whole difference between materials. The driver reads the
    terrain's workToClear as it swings.

    The expiry is generous because a shaft can be a long walk from the colony, and a job that
    expires on the way there is a colonist who never arrives.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Mine</defName>
    <driver>6</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>2</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    Carrying material to a site. It trains construction rather than hauling, which is the
    one decision in this def: the alternative is that a colony builds its first hut and
    comes out of it with four expert haulers and no builder, because most of the labour in
    a wall is the walk. The carry is the work, exactly as it is for a haul, so the
    experience is bounded the same way.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Deliver</defName>
    <driver>7</driver>
    <expiryTicks>6000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>3</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    No workTicks: building is priced per thing and per material, and
    ConstructionContent.WorkFor reads both as the colonist swings, so a stone wall is 1.7
    times the work of a wooden one. One constant here would make every wall cost the same,
    which is the whole difference between materials - the argument Job_Mine already makes.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Build</defName>
    <driver>8</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>3</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    No workTicks: what it costs to pull a thing down is derived from what it cost to raise,
    clamped to [20, 3000] ticks (a-04 section 1), so a thing that is expensive to build is slow
    to demolish without anyone maintaining two numbers that have to agree. The same argument
    Job_Mine and Job_Build already make, arrived at from the other end.

    It trains construction, not mining. Taking a wall apart without wrecking what it is made of
    is the builder's knowledge, and the colonist who put it up is the one who knows where it
    comes apart.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Deconstruct</defName>
    <driver>9</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>3</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    The two ends of the crop, U47 (docs/design/22-growing.md section 6). Neither carries
    workTicks: sowing and reaping are priced per plant, and the plant defs already carry the
    numbers (170 to break ground, 200 to cut a ripe row) - the same argument Job_Mine makes,
    that one constant here would make every crop cost the same.

    Breaking ground includes clearing what the meadow grows there already; the terrain itself
    is left alone, so there is no separate clearing job to price (a-08 section 4: the
    reference folds clearing into sowing for the same reason).

    Expiry is generous like mining's, because a zone can be a long walk from the pantry and a
    job that expires on the way there is a field nobody ever sows.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Sow</defName>
    <driver>10</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>4</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Harvest</defName>
    <driver>11</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>4</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    The draft (design 33 section 2c). A drafted colonist is always doing one of these two: standing
    where it was put, or walking to where it was sent. Neither expires - the hold ends when the
    draft's quiet hours run out, the move when it arrives - and neither may be taken away by a
    casual think, because the player put them there.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_DraftHold</defName>
    <driver>12</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Goto</defName>
    <driver>13</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    Power (design 32). Laying a line fetches its one wood and lays it in one job, priced by
    ConstructionContent.WorkFor like any build, so there is no workTicks here - Job_Build's argument.
    Taking one up is priced like deconstructing. Both train construction and settle as building
    does. Refuelling is a carry, and trains and prices as hauling does.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_LayConduit</defName>
    <driver>14</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>3</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_RemoveConduit</defName>
    <driver>15</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>3</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Refuel</defName>
    <driver>16</driver>
    <expiryTicks>6000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>0</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    The combat line (design 33 section 5), claimed together by its contracts step so that no two
    lanes can each append a job and both call it 14 (renumbered to 17-21, after power's three, at the merge with main). The drivers are stubs that fail until their
    lane fills them; nothing starts any of these yet.

    None may be taken away by a casual think: a fight, a flight, a body on the ground, a weapon
    being fetched on the player's word and a colonist being carried to bed are all things a
    better-looking job must not interrupt.

    Job_AttackMelee trains Skill_Melee (5), but per SWING and not per tick of work: the weapon's
    experiencePerSwing is the amount (Combat.xml, Items.xml), which is why experiencePerWorkTick
    is nought here. It never expires; the chase ends when one of the two goes down.

    Job_Equip and Job_Rescue carry the generous walking expiry the other working jobs have, for
    their reason: a weapon or a patient can be a long walk away.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_AttackMelee</defName>
    <driver>17</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <trainsSkill>5</trainsSkill>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Flee</defName>
    <driver>18</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <expiryTicks>1200</expiryTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Downed</defName>
    <driver>19</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Equip</defName>
    <driver>20</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <expiryTicks>6000</expiryTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Rescue</defName>
    <driver>21</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    A bandit with nobody to fight and nothing to break carries off the nearest stack it can lift
    and walks off the board's edge with it (design 33 section 17; owner, 2026-09-24: "thieve
    items"). No expiry: an expiry ends the job, and ending it drops the load, so a long walk to the
    edge would put the loot down and pick it up again. It looks up on the combat's rechooseTicks
    instead. Trains nothing: a bandit has no work to get better at.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Steal</defName>
    <driver>22</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    Medical supplies (design 37), after Job_Steal at the merge with main (renumbered 22-23 ->
    23-24; bandits shipped first). Job_Treat is a doctor fetching one unit (or none, a bare
    dressing) and treating a patient, or a colonist treating herself: workTicks is one treatment
    at the standard pace (600, INVENTED), which Work_Doctor's curve scales and self-treatment
    triples. It trains medicine at growing's rate. Not casually interruptible: a doctor halfway
    through a dressing finishes it.

    Job_Patient is a hurt colonist lying in a bed (or on the ground, when there is none) until
    she is back to the release line. She sleeps there, so rest and the bed's heal both run.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Treat</defName>
    <driver>23</driver>
    <casuallyInterruptible>false</casuallyInterruptible>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <workTicks>600</workTicks>
    <trainsSkill>6</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
  </JobDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Patient</defName>
    <driver>24</driver>
  </JobDef>

  <!--
    Picking a berry bush (design 45 section 6), driver 25: after medical
    supplies' Treat and Patient at the merge with main, which shipped them first. No workTicks: the work is the plant's own, in
    World/WildPlants.xml, as felling's is. Growing's skill and experience, because it is picking
    a crop.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Job_Forage</defName>
    <driver>25</driver>
    <expiryTicks>12000</expiryTicks>
    <trainsSkill>4</trainsSkill>
    <experiencePerWorkTick>110</experiencePerWorkTick>
    <settleTicks>30</settleTicks>
  </JobDef>

</Defs>
